---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import ProjectStory from "../../components/ProjectStory.astro";
import { ClientRouter } from "astro:transitions";

const slides = [
  {text: "The cool CGM tells us how galaxies keep forming stars or stop forming stars.",},
    {text: "But simulations show that the CGM is very complex. <br> We need observations to test these models.",},
    {text: "The problem is, the CGM is very thin. <br> We cannot see it directly. <br> So, how can we study it?",},
    {text: "One way is quasar absorption spectroscopy. <br> We use a galaxy in the front and a quasar behind it. <br> The distance between them is called the impact parameter.",},
    {text: "When the quasar light passes through the CGM, the gas absorbs some of the light. <br> This creates absorption lines in the quasar spectrum. <br> One useful line is Mg II.",},
    {text: "Mg II is strong and is often used to study the cool CGM in the optical range. <br> In the optical regime, ground-based telescopes provide large datasets.",},
    {text: "But Mg II is in the UV.",},
    {text: "At higher redshifts, the Mg II line shifts into the optical range. <br> Then, we can use ground-based telescopes. <br> These studies can find about 10,000 galaxy-quasar pairs.",},
    {text: "For low-redshift galaxies, Mg II is hard to observe from the ground. <br> Usually, we need space telescopes but the observations are expensive. <br> So, these studies usually have only about 100 galaxy-quasar pairs.",},
    {text: "But we want to study the CGM at low redshift with a much larger sample. <br> Luckily, there is another line: Ca II. <br> Ca II is already in the optical range.",},
    {text: "There is still one problem. <br> Ca II is very weak. <br> It is usually hidden by noise in a single quasar spectrum.",},
    {text: "To solve this problem, we use DESI. <br> It is an optical survey. <br> DESI collects tens of millions of spectra.",},
    {text: "It also has many quasars and bright galaxies at low redshift. <br> By matching the galaxies and quasars, we get about 900,000 pairs.",},
    {text: "But we still cannot see Ca II clearly in one spectrum. <br> So we stack the spectra at different impact parameters. <br> This makes the signal stronger and the noise smaller.",},
    {text: "Now we can ask our main question: \"How does the cool CGM vary with galaxies at low redshifts?\" <br> To answer this, we split the galaxies into different groups.",},
    {text: "The y-axis shows the strength of Ca II absorption. <br> The x-axis shows the impact parameter. <br><br> For star-forming galaxies, the absorption gets weaker as the impact parameter gets larger.",},
    {text: "We also see that more massive galaxies have stronger absorption.",},
    {text: "Because DESI has a huge sample, we can study this over a very large mass range. <br> Our range covers about three orders of magnitude in stellar mass.",},
    {text: "For quiescent galaxies, the Ca II absorption does not change much with stellar mass.",},
    {text: "So far, we have looked at the distance from the galaxy in physical units. <br> But bigger galaxies usually live in bigger halos. <br> Smaller galaxies live in smaller halos.",},
    {text: "So we divide the impact parameter by the virial radius. <br> Then we do the same measurement again at the normalized distance.",},
    {text: "Now something interesting happens. <br> For star-forming galaxies, the mass dependence disappears. <br> This means the CGM intrinsically depends on the halo.",},
    {text: "We see a similar result at higher redshift using MgII. <br> This suggests that the same process may be working over a long time.",},
    {text: "For quiescent galaxies, we see a small opposite trend. <br> More massive halos have slightly weaker Ca II absorption.",},
    {text: "There are a few possible reasons. <br> More massive galaxies can live in denser environments. In these environments, ram pressure can remove cool gas from satellite galaxies. <br> Also, bigger halos are hotter. The gas takes longer to cool. So less cool gas can form.",},
    {text: "Next, we estimate how much Ca II is in the CGM. <br> We do this by integrating the area under the best-fit relation.",},
    {text: "We find that the Ca II mass increases with stellar mass.",},
    {text: "But there is one more thing to consider. <br> Some metals in the CGM can be depleted onto dust grains. So we correct for this effect. <br> We also calculate the calcium mass in the ISM. <br> The two show a similar trend.",},
    {text: "This gives us a picture of the baryon cycle. <br> It shows that the gas and metals in galaxies and their CGM are closely connected.",},
    {text: "We also look at the direction of the CGM. <br> Previous studies found stronger absorption along the poles of galaxies. <br> This may be caused by galactic outflows.",},
    {text: "So we divide edge-on star-forming galaxies into three directions: disk",},
    {text: "So we divide edge-on star-forming galaxies into three directions: disk, intermediate",},
    {text: "So we divide edge-on star-forming galaxies into three directions: disk, intermediate, and pole.",},
    {text: "We also include face-on galaxies for comparison.",},
    {text: "But surprisingly, we find no clear difference. <br> The absorption is similar in all directions.",},
    {text: "Why? <br> One possible reason is that galactic outflows become weaker over time. <br> Both the star formation rate and outflow speed decrease from high to low redshift.",},
    {text: "Taken together, less gas mass is expected to be distributed at smaller distances at low redshifts.",},
    {text: "Our large DESI sample lets us study the cool CGM at low redshift in much more detail. <br> In the end, we are getting closer to understanding the baryon cycle of galaxies."},
];
---

  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <title>Yu Voon Ng</title>
    <!-- <ClientRouter /> -->
  </head>


  <body class="project-page">

    <header class="project-nav">
      <a href="/" class="back-link">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M21 12H3M9 6l-6 6 6 6" />
        </svg>
        Yu Voon Ng
      </a>
    </header>


    <main>

      <!-- STORY -->

        <ProjectStory
          title="A Comprehensive Characterization of Galaxy–cool CGM Connections at z < 0.4 with DESI Year 1 Data"
          publication="<strong>Ng, Yu Voon</strong>, Lan, T.-W., Prochaska, J. X., Saintonge, A., Chang, Y.-L., Siudek, M., et al., 2025, ApJ, 993, 92"
          publicationUrl="https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae0613"
          slides={slides}
          keynote="/presentations/cgm_web/index.html"
          conclusion="Our results provide a comprehensive characterization of galaxy-cool CGM connections below z<0.4 down to 10<sup>8</sup> M<sub>⨀</sub> galaxies, offering novel constraints on the models of galaxy evolution. Combined with higher-redshift measurements, these relationships have been mapped over ∼10 Gyr, providing essential insight into the underlying mechanisms driving galaxy evolution across cosmic time. <br><br> Further associations with galaxies, including AGN activity, star formation rates, redshift evolution and calcium masses, are also discussed in detail in the paper."
        />

    </main>

  </body>
